{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>As HaShem commanded Moses, so they did.</b> One must know why the verse altered this statement from the usual order, where it places the deed first and afterward says ״as [HaShem] commanded,״ whereas here it put the statement <b>as</b> etc. first and afterward said <b>so they did</b>?" "<b>And it appears</b> that since HaShem granted an advantage to the daughters of Tzelophehad and elevated them, and permitted them to marry any man, thereby removing them from the general category of the commandment, the verse comes to inform us that even though HaShem placed the choice in their hands, nevertheless they did nothing but in accordance with the King's command to the nation of Israel — and that is His saying <b>as [HaShem] commanded</b> etc., meaning: like the general commandment, so they did, and not in accordance with the permission He gave them. Therefore, had it said ״and the daughters of Tzelophehad did… as [HaShem] commanded,״ it would have been heard as though the information came to state the deed they performed, informing us through the statement ״as HaShem commanded״ in the usual order; but His stating first <b>as HaShem commanded</b> and afterward the information of the deed — this declares that it did not come to inform us of the deed but rather to negate the permission that runs counter to the general commandment. Understand this." "<b>The portion of Masei is concluded. The book of Numbers is completed.</b>"], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 36, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:17:23.560138467Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}